---
// Lesson 4.4a — Attack: Close-Range Danger
// Collision-based damage with the damage cooldown; TryDamage.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.4a — Attack: Close-Range Danger</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The enemy closes in — and now it has to actually hurt you. Contact
    damage sounds trivial ("touch = −1") until you remember Update runs
    60 times a second. The fix is the cooldown pattern from
    <a href="3.3a.html">3.3a</a>, wearing a new coat.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Read the attack half of EnemyController.cs:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private int damage = 1;
private float damageCooldown = 1.0f;
private float lastDamageTime = -1.0f;

private void OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D collision)
{
    if (collision.gameObject.CompareTag("Player"))
    {
        TryDamage();
    }
}

private void OnCollisionStay2D(Collision2D collision)
{
    if (collision.gameObject.CompareTag("Player"))
    {
        TryDamage();
    }
}

private void TryDamage()
{
    if (Time.time &gt; lastDamageTime + damageCooldown)
    {
        if (StatManager.manager != null)
        {
            StatManager.manager.ChangeHealth(-damage);
            lastDamageTime = Time.time;
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Predict: you get grabbed and cannot escape — you stand inside
        the enemy for 5 seconds. How many hits total? (Careful —
        Enter fires once; Stay fires continuously. Which one is
        doing the work after the first second?) And why does
        lastDamageTime start at <strong>−1.0</strong> instead of 0
        — what would the very first frame of a collision do
        differently?
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Turret cooldown = fire slower. Enemy cooldown = hurt
            slower. Same pattern, two meanings. What does a designer
            express with damageCooldown that they cannot express with
            damage alone — and what would "cooldown 0.1, damage 1"
            feel like vs "cooldown 1.0, damage 1"?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — rebuild TryDamage.</strong> Comment out its
    body and retype it: is the cooldown expired? → does the manager
    exist? → pay the damage, start the cooldown. The −1.0 seed means
    "the last hit happened 1 second before the scene began" — the
    very first touch connects immediately, no free grace period.
    Change it to 0 and walk into the enemy on the first frame: feel
    the one-second immunity you just invented. Restore −1.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — Enter vs Stay.</strong> Both handlers call
    TryDamage — with the cooldown, only the first tick of each second
    matters. Comment out OnCollisionStay2D's body: you take ONE hit
    per bump instead of one per second of contact. Now comment out
    OnCollisionEnter2D instead: standing on the enemy still drains
    you at 1/second. Which handler is load-bearing for a chase
    enemy — and which for a floor trap? Argue it.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — connect the whole animal.</strong> Trace a
    full encounter out loud, with the fields: spawn → patrol
    (direction ±1) → you enter 5 units → chase (normalized pursuit) →
    contact → TryDamage (cooldown gate) → you flee past 5 → the chase
    if stops matching → patrol resumes. Every sentence is a field or
    a comparison. If you can narrate it, you own it — and tomorrow
    (<a href="4.5a.html">4.5a</a>) you will draw it.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The unfair grapple</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Set damage to 3 with health 3. What is the entire
                encounter now — and why is "one-hit kill" a DESIGN
                statement, not a bug?</li>
            <li>Make TryDamage ignore the cooldown (always true).
                Stand in the enemy for 3 seconds at 60fps. How many
                hits did you take — and what happened to the death
                loop from <a href="2.2a.html">2.2a</a>?</li>
            <li>Change the tag check to "Player2". Why is silence the
                symptom again — and how long does it take you to
                find a silent tag bug NOW?</li>
            <li>The enemy damages you; you cannot damage it. What
                single system from <a href="2.1a.html">2.1a</a> would
                you borrow to give the player a counterattack — and
                why will Unit 8's boss need exactly that?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Attack design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Knockback: should the enemy push you away on contact?
                What physics component would you nudge — and why does
                the turret need nothing similar?</li>
            <li>Design a damage TIER: enemy near a turret deals 2,
                lone enemy deals 1. Where must the difference live so
                each prefab instance can differ?</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"4.4a: contact damage + cooldown"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Rebuild TryDamage and explain the −1.0 seed</li>
            <li>Explain Enter vs Stay and which behavior needs which</li>
            <li>Narrate a full encounter as fields and comparisons</li>
            <li>Explain cooldown as a fairness tool, not a nerf</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/index.html">Unit 4: The Enemy</a> — the complete enemy</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — the contact damage reference</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="4.5a.html">4.5a — The Full State Machine: Back to Patrol</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.4a" title="4.4a Attack: Close-Range Danger — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
